13.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {E (((1,4),(2,3)),5)

Re-scoring the matrix with simple-eye presence as the root state (supported by its occurrence in three of the five taxa plus the outgroup comparison to 5) produces a clean optimization on E. Under this polarity the single loss of simple eyes occurs once on the terminal branch to 3, glabrous cuticle and serrate antennae are each gained once on the (1,4) stem, antennae are lost once on 5, and claws are gained independently on 2 and 4—totaling seven steps with no extra reversals required for surface texture.  

C, by contrast, forces an additional reversal of the simple-eye state back to absent on the branch to 3 plus a second independent claw change to accommodate nesting 2 inside the glabrous clade, pushing its length to eight or nine. The two unambiguous synapomorphies uniting 1+4 (glabrous + serrate) and the shared setose condition linking 2+3 therefore remain most economically explained on E without inflating homoplasy elsewhere in the matrix.}

11.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {E (((1,4),(2,3)),5)

The five binary characters are surface (glabrous in 1+4), simple eye (present in 1+2+4), antennae (absent only in 5), serrate antennae (in 1+4), and claws. With claws treated as plesiomorphic (retained from the biramous-appendage ancestor) and all other ancestral states setose/no simple eye/antennae present/not serrate, E requires exactly 6 Fitch steps: glabrous gain once on (1+4), serrate gain once on (1+4), simple-eye gains on (1+4) and on 2, antennae loss on 5, and claws loss once on (1+4).  

C forces either two extra claw changes (independent losses on 1 and 3) or a simple-eye reversal on 3 plus an additional surface reversal, reaching at least 8 steps under the same polarity. All other options exceed 7 steps. Thus E is the unique shortest tree among those listed.}

